---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="The Missing Link — Indiana Missing Persons Directory | Silver Lining of Hope"
  description="The Missing Link is building a searchable directory of Indiana's 1,000+ missing persons — compiled from NamUs, Doe Network, Charley Project, and public records."
  heading="The Missing Link"
  intro="One clean, complete, public record of Indiana's missing — because you can't find people who fell through the cracks in the data."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Programs', href: '/programs' },
    { label: 'The Missing Link', href: '/programs/the-missing-link' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="container section--dark tml-band">
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="stat">
        <strong>1,000+</strong>
        <span>Indiana missing person cases being compiled and verified</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <strong>4 sources+</strong>
        <span>NamUs, Doe Network, Charley Project, and FOIA/APRA records</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <strong>5 states</strong>
        <span>Indiana first, then Ohio, Illinois, Michigan &amp; Kentucky</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container--narrow section prose">
    <h2>The problem: the data itself is missing</h2>
    <p>
      There is no single, complete list of Indiana's missing. NamUs holds
      some cases. The Doe Network and the Charley Project hold others. Local
      agencies hold records that never reach any national database. Names are
      spelled differently across systems, dates conflict, and some people
      appear nowhere at all. A missing person who isn't in the data is a
      missing person no one is looking for — and an unidentified person who
      can't be matched is a family that never gets an answer.
    </p>

    <h2>What we're building</h2>
    <p>
      The Missing Link is a long-term data project to compile, clean, and
      cross-reference every available record on missing persons in Indiana:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Compile</strong> — pulling every Indiana case from NamUs, the Doe Network, the Charley Project, and agency records obtained through Indiana's Access to Public Records Act.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean</strong> — reconciling names, dates, locations, and case numbers so each person has one accurate, verified entry.</li>
      <li><strong>Cross-reference</strong> — connecting missing person records with unidentified persons listings to surface potential matches for investigators.</li>
      <li><strong>Map</strong> — organizing every case by Indiana State Police district, so patterns and coverage gaps become visible.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish</strong> — a free, public, searchable directory of Indiana's missing, built to help families, researchers, and law enforcement alike.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Built with respect</h2>
    <p>
      Every entry in this directory is a person, and every record we touch
      belongs to a family's worst chapter. We publish only information that
      is already public, we honor family requests about how their loved one
      appears, and we correct errors fast. The directory is a tool for
      finding people — never content for consuming them.
    </p>

    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p>
      Indiana is phase one. As the methodology proves out, we'll extend the
      project to Ohio, Illinois, Michigan, and Kentucky — because missing
      people cross state lines, and the data should too. Progress updates are
      posted on <a href="/blog">the blog</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Help us build it</h2>
    <p>
      The Missing Link runs on volunteer researchers and donor support for
      records fees and hosting.
      <a href="/get-involved/volunteer">Volunteer your research hours</a>,
      <a href="/donate">fund a records request</a>, or — if you're with a law
      enforcement agency — <a href="/get-help/law-enforcement">partner with
      us</a> on district-level data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <style>
    .tml-band {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: none;
      padding-block: var(--space-6);
      margin-top: var(--space-5);
    }
  </style>
</PageLayout>
